   "text": {
    "L1": "India has great geographical diversity. Its major regions include mountains, plains, desert, plateau, coastal plains and islands.",
    "L2": "The Thar is dry and sandy, but it is not empty. People, animals and plants live there and adapt to little water.",
    "L3": "The Himalayas are young, very high fold mountains. Snow- and glacier-fed rivers such as the Ganga and Yamuna begin there and flow all year.",
    "L4": "The Northern Plains are flat and fertile because rivers spread alluvial soil. These conditions support farming and a large population.",
    "L5": "The Peninsular Plateau is old and made of hard rock. Most of its big rivers flow east to the Bay of Bengal.",
    "L6": "Land and climate shape ways of life. They influence crops, houses, clothes, food and travel.",
    "L7": "Kerala is warm, wet and near the sea, so people often fish and grow coconuts. High, cold and dry Ladakh suits yaks, goats and barley.",
    "L8": "India’s physical regions are different, and people adapt to the land where they live."
   },
